# callable-contracts.bot -- completion facts through callables, as a
# source-level specification (STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md, "Precision and the
# erased-callable contract"; VERIFIED-CALLABLE-CONTRACT-BOUNDARY.md;
# EXACT-CALLABLE-CLOSED-CALLER.md):
#
# * A callable that declares errors never loses them on the way through an
#   untyped helper (`fn call(f, x): f(x)`): an exact callable argument is
#   recovered at the call (its own errors, narrowed by the facts at hand), a
#   parameter or capture is charged where it was passed, and any other
#   callee is charged the program's erased-callable contract (every error of
#   every callable the program passes into an untyped parameter) and falls
#   back to the callee's declared contract. Along every path a wrong handler
#   is UnhandledError at the handled call, and the right ones are accepted
#   and run, on every backend, with exactly the identities the analysis
#   allowed. A fallback is a may-set: never KnownError, never hiding one.
# * A call through a structural Fn or Coroutine type is bounded by that
#   type's contract, whatever callable it holds and whatever the program
#   erases elsewhere: a handler for the contract's errors is enough, and an
#   enclosing function must admit exactly those. The bound is sound because
#   a value acquiring the type is checked against it: a closure that may
#   raise outside the contract through a callable it captured or receives is
#   a Type error where it acquires the type.
# * Exact callables passed through a helper's parameter -- Botlish blocks, a
#   native, a value-capturing closure, the refined-checks scan -- compile to
#   code every backend agrees on.
#
# Ported from tests/higher-order-completions.test (hoc-*),
# tests/structural-contract-completions.test (scc-*) and
# tests/exact-callable-executable.test (exact-callable-executable-*). The Tcl
# tests stay authoritative; what only they observe stays there: the
# analysis' effective-error stamps (hir::completions::effectiveErrorsOf), the
# erased-callable contract itself, affine error edges, specialization
# clones, every diagnostic of a program after its first (a rejected block
# reifies one), allocation reports, and the standalone AOT executable.
#
# The erased-callable contract belongs to the whole program, and an accepted
# block is part of the program: every error any block here erases is charged
# to every call in the suite that has neither an identity nor a contract.
# What each test here observes does not depend on which other errors the
# program erases: its calls are exact, recovered or contracted, or its
# outcome is a rejection those errors cannot supply or change. The few whose
# Tcl program is accepted only because it erases exactly what it erases are
# in suites of their own: callable-contracts-returned.bot (a returned
# closure's call, charged the contract, with handlers for hocDecls' errors
# only), callable-contracts-fallback.bot (hocWrapper's functions, which
# admit only E1 and E3), callable-contracts-handle.bot (a coroutine
# construction given an untyped parameter, in a function admitting only E)
# and callable-contracts-unerased.bot (a program that erases nothing). So
# are the two whose outcome another block's erasure could supply:
# sccContravariant's rejection and the charge of its accepted call are E1
# put there by contravariant erasure alone, while C1, its name here, is
# erased directly by blocks of this suite
# (callable-contracts-contravariant-rejected.bot and
# callable-contracts-contravariant.bot).
#
# Static decisions. Nearly every block here both erases (it passes an
# error-declaring callable to an untyped helper: call, outer, make, rec, ...)
# and consumes the erased-callable contract (the helpers' generic bodies call
# their untyped parameters), so the decision scheduler decides the rejected
# ones one per build: 26 rounds and 30 builds on this tree (reported with the
# migration; the layout is the natural one and is not bent to avoid it).
#
# Driver inputs. Where a Tcl program's driver (its top-level expression)
# chooses with a literal what a run raises, the literal is an argument of
# the registration function, captured by the body, so the error is raised
# and handled at run time on every backend (values.bot's convention).
# Literals inside the programs' own functions, and the drivers of programs
# whose theorem is static, are the programs' own.
#
# Imports:
#   compiler        std::compiler::CompilerError and DiagnosticCode (every body)
#   coroutine       the coroutine programs (hocCoroutine, sccAffine,
#                   sccAffinePayload, sccCoroutine, sccHandle import it)
#   immutable_set   the refined-checks scan's local character set
#   list            std::list::at in the value-capturing exact closure
#   mutable_array   std::mutable_array::generate (hoc-mutable-array-factory,
#                   scc-native-factory) and its `.capacity()`
#   mutable_vector  scc's MutableVector transport (no_jobs, vector_first)
#   str             std::str::length in the payload handlers; std::str::substring,
#                   std::str::concat and the exact natives std::str::is_tcl_alpha /
#                   std::str::is_tcl_alnum of the exact-callable programs
#   test            the framework
import std::compiler
import std::coroutine
import std::immutable_set
import std::list
import std::mutable_array
import std::mutable_vector
import std::str
import std::test

# ===========================================================================
# higher-order-completions.test: completion analysis through untyped
# callables degrades conservatively.

# Three errors (hocDecls): a payload-free one and two payload-bearing twins
# with the same fields. hocAlways, hocMaybe and hocCoroutine declare the
# same E1 (hocMaybe the same E2): their ports here use these (hocWrapper's
# are callable-contracts-fallback.bot's).
error E1

error E2:
    code: int

error E3:
    code: int

# The payload-bearing reproducer's errors (hocOriginalPayload's E1 and E2,
# renamed: E1 and E2 above are declared differently).
error OriginE1:
    origin: str

error OriginE2:
    origin: str

# The messages of the coroutine programs (hocCoroutine; sccAffine and
# sccCoroutine declare the same two structs).
struct Message:
    value: int

struct Event:
    value: int

# The file's shared prelude (hocDecls), at the top level as in every program
# of the Tcl file: raise fails E1, E2 or E3 according to its selector, call
# is the untyped helper, and the others pass raise on along the paths
# below. generic and partial hand raise to call: the program erases E1, E2
# and E3.
fn raise(x) -> int errors E1, E2, E3:
    if x == 1:
        fail E1
    if x == 2:
        fail E2 {code: 20}
    if x == 3:
        fail E3 {code: 30}
    0

fn call(f, x):
    f(x)

fn generic(x: int) -> int errors E1, E2, E3:
    call(raise, x)

fn g1(x: int) -> int errors E1, E2, E3:
    generic(x)

fn g2(x: int) -> int errors E1, E2, E3:
    g1(x)

fn partial(x: int) -> int errors E1, E3:
    call(raise, x):
        on E2 {code}:
            code + 1

fn outer(f, x):
    fn inner(y):
        f(y)
    inner(x)

fn make(f):
    fn inner(y):
        f(y)
    inner

fn use(kf: Fn{args: [int], return: any, errors: [E1, E2, E3]}, x: int) errors E1, E2, E3:
    kf(x)

fn rec(f, n: int, x):
    if n < 1:
        f(x)
    else:
        rec(f, n - 1, x)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A8: the original reproducer, payload-bearing (errors.bot ports the
# payload-free one: hoc-original-payload-free).

fn register_hoc_original_tests(k0: int, k1: int, k2: int):
    # hoc-original-payload: OriginE1 escapes generic through call, so a
    # handler for OriginE2 alone is UnhandledError -- in bad, at generic(x)
    # (the location), for OriginE1 (the identity, which only the message
    # names).
    fn original_payload() errors std::compiler::CompilerError:
        static compile:
            fn raise(x) -> int errors OriginE1, OriginE2:
                if x == 1:
                    fail OriginE1 {origin: "one"}
                if x == 2:
                    fail OriginE2 {origin: "two"}
                0
            fn call(f, x):
                f(x)
            fn generic(x) -> int errors OriginE1, OriginE2:
                call(raise, x)
            fn bad(x):
                generic(x):
                    on OriginE2 {origin}:
                        -1 + 0 * std::str::length(origin)
            0
    std::test::rejects_at("hoc-original-payload", std::compiler::DiagnosticCode::UnhandledError, 196, 17, original_payload)
    std::test::rejects_containing("hoc-original-payload/identity", std::compiler::DiagnosticCode::UnhandledError, "declared error \"OriginE1\"", original_payload)

    # hoc-original-payload-both-handled: with both handled it is accepted,
    # and each payload reaches its handler.
    fn original_payload_both() errors std::compiler::CompilerError:
        static compile:
            fn raise(x) -> int errors OriginE1, OriginE2:
                if x == 1:
                    fail OriginE1 {origin: "one"}
                if x == 2:
                    fail OriginE2 {origin: "two"}
                0
            fn call(f, x):
                f(x)
            fn generic(x) -> int errors OriginE1, OriginE2:
                call(raise, x)
            fn bad(x):
                generic(x):
                    on OriginE1 {origin}:
                        -2 + 0 * std::str::length(origin)
                    on OriginE2 {origin}:
                        -1 + 0 * std::str::length(origin)
            [bad(k0), bad(k1), bad(k2)]
    std::test::value("hoc-original-payload-both-handled", [0, -2, -1], original_payload_both)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A9 and A11: the wrong handler, and the right ones, along every path
# (hocPaths). observe(k) makes one handled call, k selecting what raise
# does. Each path is tested four ways:
#
#   hoc-wrong-handler-PATH       handlers for E2 and E3 only: E1 unhandled
#   hoc-wrong-twin-PATH          handlers for E1 and E2 only: the payload-
#                                bearing E3 is not handled by its same-shaped
#                                twin's handler
#   hoc-wrong-twin-reverse-PATH  handlers for E1 and E3 only: E2 unhandled
#   hoc-all-handled-PATH         every error handled (in the order E3, E1,
#                                E2): accepted, and the value shows each
#                                identity raised and caught on every backend
#                                -- also hoc-differential-PATH's observable
#                                half (its other half, that each observed
#                                identity is in the analysis' effective
#                                errors, stays in Tcl)
#
# The three wrong ones are rejected UnhandledError (the Tcl tests compare the
# kind only; observe declares nothing, so the unhandled error leaves it and
# is diagnosed at the driver's first call). The returned path (a closure
# returned by make: its call is charged the erased-callable contract) is
# callable-contracts-returned.bot's.

# direct: call(raise, k) -- the exact argument is recovered at the call.
fn register_hoc_direct_tests(k0: int, k1: int, k2: int, k3: int):
    fn wrong_handler() errors std::compiler::CompilerError:
        static compile:
            fn observe(k: int):
                r = call(raise, k):
                    on E2 {code}:
                        return ["E2", code]
                    on E3 d:
                        return ["E3", d.code]
                [r]
            [observe(k0), observe(k1), observe(k2), observe(k3)]
    std::test::rejects("hoc-wrong-handler-direct", std::compiler::DiagnosticCode::UnhandledError, wrong_handler)

    fn wrong_twin() errors std::compiler::CompilerError:
        static compile:
            fn observe(k: int):
                r = call(raise, k):
                    on E1:
                        return ["E1"]
                    on E2 {code}:
                        return ["E2", code]
                [r]
            [observe(k0), observe(k1), observe(k2), observe(k3)]
    std::test::rejects("hoc-wrong-twin-direct", std::compiler::DiagnosticCode::UnhandledError, wrong_twin)

    fn wrong_twin_reverse() errors std::compiler::CompilerError:
        static compile:
            fn observe(k: int):
                r = call(raise, k):
                    on E1:
                        return ["E1"]
                    on E3 d:
                        return ["E3", d.code]
                [r]
            [observe(k0), observe(k1), observe(k2), observe(k3)]
    std::test::rejects("hoc-wrong-twin-reverse-direct", std::compiler::DiagnosticCode::UnhandledError, wrong_twin_reverse)

    fn all_handled() errors std::compiler::CompilerError:
        static compile:
            fn observe(k: int):
                r = call(raise, k):
                    on E3 d:
                        return ["E3", d.code]
                    on E1:
                        return ["E1"]
                    on E2 {code}:
                        return ["E2", code]
                [r]
            [observe(k0), observe(k1), observe(k2), observe(k3)]
    std::test::value("hoc-all-handled-direct", [[0], ["E1"], ["E2", 20], ["E3", 30]], all_handled)

